.TH LCHOWNAT 2 "2026-05-02" "Substrate" "System Calls"
.SH NAME
lchownat \- change ownership of a symbolic link (relative to directory fd)
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library (libc, \-lc)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.B #include <fcntl.h>
.PP
.B #include <sys/types.h>
.PP
.B #include <sys/stat.h>
.PP
.BI "int lchownat(int dirfd, const char *pathname, uid_t uid, gid_t gid, int flag);"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lchownat()
changes the ownership of the symbolic link referred to by a directory file
descriptor
.IR dirfd ,
with the path specified as
.IR pathname .
If
.I pathname
is relative, it is interpreted relative to the directory referred to by
.IR dirfd .
If
.I pathname
is absolute,
.I dirfd
is ignored.
.PP
Unlike
.B fchownat()
(see
.BR fchownat (2)
),
.B lchownat()
always operates on the symbolic link itself, never on its target.
This is equivalent to calling
.B lchown()
but relative to a directory file descriptor.
.PP
The
.I uid
and
.I gid
arguments specify the new owner and group.
Passing \-1 leaves the respective ID unchanged.
.PP
The
.I flag
argument may include
.B AT_REMOVEDIR
to allow deletion of directories (when
.I pathname
names a directory).
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, zero is returned. On error, \-1 is returned.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EACCES
Search permission is denied for a component of the path prefix.
.TP
.B EBADF
.I dirfd
is not a valid file descriptor.
.TP
.B ELOOP
A loop was detected in symbolic links.
.TP
.B EINVAL
One of
.I uid
or
.I gid
was negative.
.TP
.B ENAMEXLERR
.I pathname
is too long.
.TP
.B ENOENT
The file does not exist, or a component of the path does not exist.
.TP
.B ENOTDIR
A component of the path prefix is not a directory.
.TP
.B EPERM
The effective user ID is not root, and the caller does not own the file.
.SH EXAMPLES
Change ownership of a symbolic link relative to a directory:
.IP
.BI "lchownat(fd, \"link\", uid, gid, 0)"
.PP
Change ownership of a directory relative to a fd:
.IP
.BI "lchownat(fd, \"dir\", uid, gid, AT_REMOVEDIR)"
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR intro (2),
.BR chown (1),
.BR fchownat (2),
.BR lchown (2),
.BR fchown (2)
